    "topic": "gs2_polity",
    "topicLabel": "Indian Polity & Governance",
    "difficulty": "medium",
    "question": "A party is recognised as a National Party if any of the following conditions is fulfilled:\n1. If it secures six percent of valid votes polled in any four or more state at a general election to the Lok Sabha or to the Legislative Assembly.\n2. If it wins four seats in the Lok Sabha from any State or States.\n3. If it wins two percent of the seats in the Lok Sabha at a general election and these candidates are elected from three States.\n4. If it is recognised as a State party at least in 8 States.\nWhich of the above statement is/are correct?",
    "options": [
      "1, 2 and 4",
      "Only 4",
      "1 and 3",
      "1, 2 and 3"
    ],
    "explanation": "ECI rules recognise a party as 'National' if it meets any of: 6% votes in 4+ states plus 4 Lok Sabha seats, or 2% of Lok Sabha seats from at least 3 states, or is a recognised state party in at least 4 (not 8) states. So statements 1, 2 and 3 are correct; statement 4 wrongly says '8 States'.\n\n💡 It's a club membership with three valid ID cards (1,2,3) — the fourth card (statement 4) has the wrong number printed on it (8 instead of 4), so it doesn't count.\n\n⚠️ Statement 4 looks official and specific, tempting you to include it, but the real threshold is 4 states, not 8.",
    "source": "MPSC State Tax Officer / Group B Gazetted",
    "year": 2024,
    "paperId": "mpsc-group-b-combined-(state-tax-officer-/-cooperative-audit-officer)-2024-gs-ii",
