    "difficulty": "medium",
    "question": "What type of biological molecule acts as a catalyst, speeding up chemical reactions in living organisms?",
    "options": [
      "Enzyme",
      "Hormone",
      "Antibody",
      "Lipid"
    ],
    "explanation": "Enzymes are proteins that speed up biochemical reactions in cells without being consumed themselves.\n\n💡 An enzyme is like a matchmaker at a party — it introduces two molecules to each other so they react faster, then walks off to matchmake again, unchanged.\n\n⚠️ Hormones also 'trigger' body changes, which tempts people to confuse them with catalysts, but hormones are messengers, not reaction-speeders.",
    "source": "MPSC State Tax Officer / Group B Gazetted",
