    "topicLabel": "Indian Economy",
    "difficulty": "medium",
    "question": "With reference to India, consider the following events : (i) Nationalisation of Banks, 1969. (ii) Formation of Regional Rural Banks. 1975. (iii) Adoption of villages by Bank Branches. (iv) Liberalization of the economy. Which of the above events can be considered as steps taken to achieve ‘financial inclusion in India’?",
    "options": [
      "Only (ii) and (iii)",
      "(i), (ii) and (iii)",
      "Only (i) and (ii)",
      "(i), (ii), (iii) and (iv)"
    ],
    "explanation": "Bank nationalisation (1969), Regional Rural Banks (1975), and village adoption by bank branches were direct financial-inclusion drives; 1991 liberalisation was about opening up the economy generally, not a targeted inclusion measure.\n\n💡 It's like opening more bank branches in every neighbourhood (inclusion) versus removing trade barriers with other countries (liberalisation) — different goals entirely.\n\n⚠️ Liberalisation gets lumped in because it's a major banking-era reform too, but its aim was market opening, not reaching unbanked people.",
    "source": "MPSC State Tax Officer / Group B Gazetted",
