    "difficulty": "medium",
    "question": "What were the primary factors leading to the evolution and incorporation of the Sixth Schedule provision in the Constitution of India, which subsequently enabled the emergence of Autonomous District Councils like those in Mizoram?",
    "options": [
      "The British colonial administration’s desire to maintain control over tribal areas and exploit their resources.",
      "The tribal desire for political autonomy and their quest for economic development.",
      "The need to integrate tribal communities fully into the mainstream administrative structure of India.",
      "The recommendations of the Cabinet Mission to establish direct central rule over all tribal regions."
    ],
    "explanation": "The Sixth Schedule was incorporated mainly to accommodate tribal communities' desire for self-governance and protection of customary institutions while enabling their own path to development, leading to Autonomous District Councils like Mizoram's.\n\n💡 It's like giving certain neighborhoods their own local council to run things their own way instead of forcing one city-wide rulebook on everyone.\n\n⚠️ \"The British colonial administration's desire to maintain control\" is tempting since tribal-area special treatment did start in colonial policy, but the Sixth Schedule itself was framed post-independence for tribal autonomy and development.",
